weaver: claude-sonnet-5
judge: claude-sonnet-5
quality: 4
narrative:
  Kelnex, the scheduler, acts only once 5 of its peers countersign, and answers reads from its newest snapshot only. Nexsax, the courier, hands its sealed ledger to Kelnex — the ledger a courier delivery Kelnex requires quorum to act on. Nexsax itself acts only once 2 of its peers countersign and, like Kelnex, answers reads from its newest snapshot only, so both endpoints of the ledger handoff share the newest-snapshot-only read discipline, even as their countersignature floors differ: 5 for Kelnex, 2 for Nexsax — Kelnex is the harder gate to clear.

  Kelnex publishes its sweep verdicts to Toldor, the broker. Toldor refuses new writes while its sweep is running, so the verdicts Kelnex publishes arrive at a broker that will not accept writes mid-sweep; anything Toldor cannot claim promptly is evicted after 6 ticks. Toldor signals backpressure to Nexsax, closing the loop: Nexsax delivers ledgers to Kelnex, Kelnex's verdicts reach Toldor, and Toldor's backpressure returns to Nexsax, the courier that started the chain.

  Retry behavior binds two of the three tightly and sets the third apart: Kelnex and Toldor each retry a failed handoff at most 3 times before parking it, an identical ceiling shared across the scheduler and the broker. Nexsax retries at most 4 times before parking — a higher tolerance than either of its neighbors. So across the full cycle — Nexsax to Kelnex, Kelnex to Toldor, Toldor back to Nexsax — the system's weakest retry floor is 3 attempts, set by Kelnex and Toldor together, while Nexsax alone can absorb a fourth failure before parking its handoff.
